+## 5. Cost
+
+The build adds one pass over the entries it already holds, and no request to
+GitHub. Each entry's `members` comes from the `stack` list its page already
+carries, so the build works out no membership again.
+
+`site.json` trades each project's `reviews`, one number per review, for its
+`stacks`, one number per member of each of its stacks. A member without a
+summary adds one number. For this repository, that is tens of numbers in a
+file of about 13 kilobytes.
+
+In the browser, a project page draws at most tens of rows, each found by a
+linear search of `site.json`'s `reviews`. At 1,000 reviews, that is tens of
+thousands of comparisons, well under a millisecond. Every site page already
+loads `summary.js`, so the moved helpers add nothing to load.
+
+## 6. Rollout
+
+1. `stack-header`'s implementation merges. That plan merged in #216, and its
+   `header` field, `find_stacks`, and `stackHeaderHtml` are this plan's
+   inputs.
+2. This plan is implemented in one pull request. `site.json`'s `stacks`, the
+   page that reads it, and the compiled scripts ship together, because a
+   build copies the CLI's own assets.
+
+## 7. Background
+
+The evidence comes from this repository's site, `site.json` fetched on
+2026-10-10, and from the code on `main`:
+
+- **What project pages list today.** Of 14 projects, 5 list a review:
+  `agent-instructions` and `projector` list #182, `incremental-review` lists
+  #212, `multi-model-review` lists #214, and `summary-diff-context` lists
+  #204. The line labels each review by its summary's `name`, so #212 reads
+  `Incremental review plan`.
+- **No project page here draws the tree.** No project in this repository has
+  a supplemental file or a nested project, so the tree column appears only in
+  the tests' `alpha` fixture.
+- **How the summary sidebar is drawn.** `renderPage` in `site/src/summary.ts`
+  writes `<nav class="nav" aria-label="Sections">` with the `.prblock`, the
+  `.extra` tabs, the numbered sections, and the `.navfoot` row, inline. Its
+  `stackList` and `headsList` live inside the closure that draws one summary,
+  so `site.ts` cannot call them.
+- **Why the scoping matters.** Every site page loads `summary.css`, which is
+  why its column rules are written `.layout > .side`. `site.css` writes the
+  tree column's rules as `.sitemain .side`, and its list and link rules as
+  `.sitemain .side ul`, `.sitemain .side li`, and `.sitemain .side a`. Inside
+  a project page's main element, the first would pin the sidebar's `aside` to
+  the window's left edge at 240 pixels. The others would indent the sidebar's
+  lists by 12 pixels, space its rows 5 pixels apart, and turn its links muted,
+  because `site.css` loads after `summary.css` and its selectors are as
+  specific or more.
